Chapter: HandshakeProveGuardTest
  cites Trust chapter Handshake
  cites Trust chapter TrustLattice
  cites Foreword chapter Ed25519
  cites Foreword chapter Sha512

 Track D item 2. Before this, hs-receive-prove took no signature at all: it
 bound expected-nonce, never read it, and returned HsCompleted unconditionally
 with the trust score of whatever key the peer had claimed in its hello. Rows 2
 and 3 are the arms that were impossible to write against that version, because
 there was no input that could make it refuse.

 Each of the four guards was ablated and run. Removing the ed25519-verify call
 flips row 2 to failed=False: a signature over a different message is accepted
 with the impersonated key's trust score, which is the pre-fix behaviour.
 Removing either LENGTH check does not merely soften a refusal, it kills the
 guest: an empty signature and a 3-byte key both reach the crypto, where
 ed-list-drop computes a negative slice length and ge-from-bytes reads index 31,
 and the arm dies !EXC=06 before printing. Removing the hello key check flips
 row 6 to failed=False.

 t1-sk is RFC 8032 section 7.1 test vector 1, the same secret key
 ed25519-sign-test uses, so the key and signatures here are checkable against a
 published source rather than against our own encoder.

Section: Helpers
  show-bool : Boolean -> Text
  show-bool (b) = if b then "True" else "False"

Section: Vectors
  t1-sk : List Integer = [157, 97, 177, 157, 239, 253, 90, 96, 186, 132, 74, 244, 146, 236, 44, 196, 68, 73, 197, 105, 123, 50, 105, 25, 112, 59, 172, 3, 28, 174, 127, 96]

  hpg-nonce : List Integer = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]

  hpg-other : List Integer = [9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9]

  hpg-short-key : List Integer = [1, 2, 3]

Section: Body
  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let pk = ed25519-public-key t1-sk
    in let sig = ed25519-sign t1-sk pk hpg-nonce
    in let bad-sig = ed25519-sign t1-sk pk hpg-other
    in let lat = lattice-add-direct lattice-empty (bytes-to-hex pk) 7000
    in let r-good = hs-receive-prove hpg-nonce pk sig lat
    in let r-wrong = hs-receive-prove hpg-nonce pk bad-sig lat
    in let r-empty = hs-receive-prove hpg-nonce pk [] lat
    in let r-shortkey = hs-receive-prove hpg-nonce hpg-short-key sig lat
    in let h-good = hs-receive-hello (HelloMsg { public-key = pk, pow-nonce = [], pow-difficulty = 0 }) hpg-nonce lat 0
    in let h-short = hs-receive-hello (HelloMsg { public-key = hpg-short-key, pow-nonce = [], pow-difficulty = 0 }) hpg-nonce lat 0
    in act
      print-line-uni ("prove-good: completed=" & show-bool (hs-is-completed (r-good.state)) & " score=" & show (hs-trust-score (r-good.state)))
      print-line-uni ("prove-wrongsig: failed=" & show-bool (hs-is-failed (r-wrong.state)) & " " & hs-failure-reason (r-wrong.state))
      print-line-uni ("prove-emptysig: failed=" & show-bool (hs-is-failed (r-empty.state)) & " " & hs-failure-reason (r-empty.state))
      print-line-uni ("prove-shortkey: failed=" & show-bool (hs-is-failed (r-shortkey.state)) & " " & hs-failure-reason (r-shortkey.state))
      print-line-uni ("hello-good: failed=" & show-bool (hs-is-failed (h-good.state)))
      print-line-uni ("hello-shortkey: failed=" & show-bool (hs-is-failed (h-short.state)) & " " & hs-failure-reason (h-short.state))
    end

